## Agent Tooling

This repository is intended to be self-sufficient for agent-assisted work with
Codex, Claude Code, and OpenCode.

- `AGENTS.md` is the shared project instruction source of truth.
- `.codex/`, `.claude/`, `.opencode/`, and `.agents/` are versioned on purpose.
  They contain project-specific skills, commands, rules, and MCP wiring.
- Project configs should stay portable. Do not commit user-specific absolute
  paths, local trust state, secrets, or machine-only integrations.
- CodeGraph is available for structural code search through the configured MCP
  server. Use it for symbols, call graphs, impact checks, and module surveys.
- Skills are part of the repo contract. Do not ignore skill directories just to
  reduce search/index noise.

## First run

### Prerequisites

- **Docker** with the Compose plugin (the whole backend runs in containers).
- **[just](https://github.com/casey/just)** — command runner for every dev task.
- **Node.js 24** LTS (major pinned in `.nvmrc`).
- **pnpm 11** — `corepack enable` activates the version pinned in
  `frontend/package.json`.
- Optional: **uv** and **lefthook** for the backend pre-commit hooks
  (`lefthook install`).

The repository keeps every runtime, container, CI, engine, and `@types/node`
pin on the latest Node.js major that has officially entered LTS. A scheduled
workflow proposes future LTS migrations as reviewable pull requests; it never
merges them automatically and never selects a Current release.

Repositories created from the Copier template must enable **Allow GitHub
Actions to create and approve pull requests** under **Settings → Actions →
General → Workflow permissions**. The automation only creates the migration
PR; normal review and branch protection still apply.

### Steps

1. Create your repository from this template (or clone it) and enter it.
2. Copy the environment files:

   ```bash
   cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
   cp frontend/.env.example frontend/.env
   ```

3. Start the backend stack — docker compose brings up the API plus Postgres,
   Redis, MinIO, Mailpit, and Directus:

   ```bash
   just backend dev
   ```

4. In another terminal, apply migrations, seed demo data, and create the
   MinIO bucket (in that order):

   ```bash
   just backend migrate
   just backend seed
   just backend minio-setup
   ```

5. Install frontend dependencies and start the frontend:

   ```bash
   just frontend install
   just frontend dev
   ```

6. Log in at `http://localhost:3000` with the seeded owner:
   `[[ owner_email ]]` / `12345678x`.

### Using this as a template

**Recommended: Copier.** Generate a fully renamed project from the published
template mirror and receive future boilerplate improvements with
`copier update`:

```bash
uvx copier copy --trust gh:Llamitai/[[ project_slug ]]-template my-app
```

`--trust` is required because the template declares `_tasks` (it copies
`backend/.env.example` to `backend/.env` and runs `git init` on first copy).
After generating, regenerate the backend lockfile — the production image
installs with `uv sync --locked` and fails on the stale one:

```bash
cd my-app/backend && uv lock
```

Later, pull newer template releases from inside your project with
`uvx copier update --trust`.

The mirror is generated on every `v*` tag by
[`publish-template.yml`](.github/workflows/publish-template.yml) via
[`scripts/build_template.py`](scripts/build_template.py); branding tokens live
in [`scripts/branding_tokens.json`](scripts/branding_tokens.json). Never edit
the mirror by hand.

**Lightweight alternative: GitHub template.**

- On GitHub, enable **Settings → Template repository** so new projects can be
  created with "Use this template" instead of forking.
- To rebrand the neutral "[[ project_name ]]" naming, ask your agent to run the
  [`rename-project`](.claude/skills/rename-project) skill — it renames package
  metadata, Docker images, docs, UI metadata, and seeds in one pass (no
  `copier update` afterwards, though).
- Personalize the copyright line in [`LICENSE`](LICENSE) with your own name or
  company.

## Quick Start

```bash
just                 # list commands
just backend dev     # backend stack: API + Postgres + Redis + MinIO + Mailpit + Directus
just frontend dev    # Next.js dev server
just docs dev        # React Router 7 + Fumadocs docs site
just stop-all        # stop local stacks
```

Commands are grouped by area:

```bash
just backend
just frontend
just docs
```

## Database

The backend starts from a single core migration:

[`backend/src/common/database/versions/20260707_000001_initial_[[ python_name ]].py`](backend/src/common/database/versions/20260707_000001_initial_[[ python_name ]].py)

Run migrations with:

```bash
just backend migrate
```

## Architecture

- Backend uses Clean Architecture with DDD:
  `domain -> application -> infrastructure -> presentation`.
- Frontend uses a feature-first structure:
  `frontend/src/app -> frontend/src/features -> frontend/src/entities -> frontend/src/shared`.
- Browser code calls the backend only through same-origin Next.js BFF routes
  under `/api/...`.

See [`docs/internal/architecture/frontend-architecture.md`](docs/internal/architecture/frontend-architecture.md)
for the frontend source of truth.

